----- AI Results: ------
Added a new top-level section =Why AI-Activity, Not Chatbot?= between
=Collaboration Across People and Across Sessions= and =Overview=.
Placement rationale: practical framings (concept + model, human/AI
continuity) are established first, so the philosophical section reads
as reflection rather than manifesto, and it lands cleanly into the
concrete Overview that follows.

Iterated with user through three drafts: initial (~8 sentences,
Ghosh et al. named explicitly + full Illich unpacking), user's own
denser edit (dropped Illich unpacking, added an =AI-Activity=
identity claim), and final tight version. Final is six sentences,
one paragraph:

- Opening claim: the chatbot has quietly become the default
  interface for AI.
- Diagnostic pivot: this is not a neutral choice.
- Citation carrying epistemic weight: the paper title
  =/What if AI systems weren't chatbots?/= is the DOI link
  (=https://doi.org/10.1145/3805689.3812232=); Ghosh et al. are
  reachable through the link but not named in prose. "A
  peer-reviewed argument" flags the epistemic register without
  paraphrasing further.
- Three harms in one clause: agency erosion, deskilling, knowledge
  homogenization. Contrast: task-specific workflow-integrated systems
  largely avoid these.
- The =AI-Activity= identity claim: "our move toward /convivial
  tools/ --- shaped by their user rather than the reverse --- and
  away from the industrial default." =convivial tools= is a link to
  the Wikipedia entry for Illich's =Tools for Conviviality=, so
  readers who want the philosophical grounding can follow the link;
  the six-word unpacking ("shaped by their user rather than the
  reverse") keeps the definition on-page without a name-drop.
- Transitional close: "What follows is the concrete mechanics of
  that inversion" --- graceful hinge into Overview.

TOC entry added at the appropriate position between the two
neighbouring sections.


