- Is there some form of layering that can be applied to skills?
----- AI Results: ------

*First cut (2026-10-07); accepted the same day after the revisions below.* Not in =py3/images= but with the
other workflow figures:
=bisos/defaults/ai-templates/_nonTemplate_/images/aiCommandsSkillsTree-graphviz.pcs=
(+ all outputs), linked from =mother/AI-WORKFLOW.org=. Covers commands as
well as skills, as discussed.

*Decisions (Mohsen):*
- An unnumbered tree in the style of an object-id tree.
- Show where each leaf lives.
- Include the prerequisite edges.

*The tree* (left to right, four levels on both sides):
#+begin_example
AI-Activity commands and skills
├─ General (guiding the workflow)
│   ├─ Commands (you invoke):  session start | work loop | session end | setup   [11 /bx-*]
│   └─ Skills (Claude loads):  AI-Activity model                               [authoring-skills]
└─ Digital-ecosystem specific (BISOS / Blee / ByStar)
    └─ Skills (Claude loads):  authorship model | PyCS commands | seeded CS |
                               packaging and docs | lookups                      [17 skills]
#+end_example
- Leaf colour = where it lives in the templates tree, which is also who sees
  it: =mother/= (every project), =bisos-pip/=, =aiActivity/=, =sbom+cap/=
  (each only for that =--activity=).
- Box = command, rounded = skill.
- Purple dotted arrows = "load the target skill first". These were taken
  from the SKILL.md files, not from memory:
  - =spread-seeded= → =seeded=
  - =walkable= → =seeded= and =spread-seeded=
  - =cbm= → =spread-seeded= (and → =walkable= /if walked/, drawn
    lighter as conditional)
  - =icm-to-pycs= → =writing-cs-commands=
  - =authoring-skills= mentions =dblock-authoring= only as an example
    phrasing, not as a prerequisite, so it is excluded.

*On the original question, "is there some form of layering?":* yes, two.
1. The *purpose* tree, which is the figure's skeleton.
2. A *prerequisite* layering inside the ecosystem skills:
   =seeded-cs-commands= is the base, then =spread-seeded=, then
   =walkable= / =cbm=; =writing-cs-commands= sits under =icm-to-pycs=.

*What the figure exposes* (candidates for follow-up; not acted on):
- *Provisioning ≠ purpose.* All 16 ecosystem skills except =sbom-authoring=
  live in =bisos-pip/_skills=, including =cbm-authorship=,
  =sysd-pcs-authorship= and =walkable-tree-planting=, which aren't about pip
  packages. A project initiated with =--activity=pycs=, =sbom+cap= or
  =bisos-core= doesn't see them. Conversely, a =bisos-pip= project doesn't
  see =sbom-authoring=.
- *General guidance is carried by commands*, not skills: 11 commands vs 1
  skill.
- *A prerequisite can be unreachable.* A skill is only usable if its
  prerequisites are provisioned in the same activity. Today they all are,
  because everything is in =bisos-pip/=, but that breaks as soon as skills
  move. Any re-homing (e.g. a shared ecosystem-skills set that several
  activities link to) must move prerequisite chains together.

*Second cut (same day): split into two graphs* (Mohsen: "too much for one
graph"). The module was renamed =aiCommandsSkillsTrees-graphviz.pcs= and
holds two named graphs:
- =aiGeneralTree=: General → Commands (4 session-phase groups, 11 /bx-*) +
  Skills (=authoring-skills=). Legend: =mother/=, =aiActivity/=.
- =aiEcosystemTree=: the digital-ecosystem skills root → 5 area groups → 17
  skills, plus the prerequisite edges. Legend: =bisos-pip/=, =sbom+cap/=.
  The commands/skills level is dropped, since every leaf is a skill.
Shared helpers (=_treeNew=, =_subtree=, =_legend=) and one =lives= table
keep the two figures consistent. Each legend lists only the locations that
occur in its figure. The old single-graph outputs were removed (untracked),
and the =mother/AI-WORKFLOW.org= pointer now names both PNGs. The tree
outline above still describes the content; it is now drawn as two figures.

*Layout notes:* left to right (29 leaves would be far too wide top to
bottom). The first render had the ecosystem leaves one level shallower
than the general ones. Fixed by an explicit "Skills (Claude loads)" level
under the ecosystem branch, which is also accurate, since every ecosystem
leaf is a skill. The legend is anchored below the last group so it doesn't
split the branch edges.

